16:14:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very logical question, and it cuts to the heart of why pilots and meteorologists talk about storms differently. The short answer is: **Pilots don’t mention the extreme cold because, at the altitudes they fly, that kind of cold is absolutely normal, routine, and completely unremarkable—even in the middle of summer.** Here’s the breakdown. ### 1. Standard Atmospheric Temperature at Cruise Altitude Commercial jets typically cruise between 30,000 and 40,000 feet. In the International Standard Atmosphere, the temperature at 35,000 feet is **-54°C (-65°F)**. That’s not a storm value; that’s just the average, calm, sunny-day temperature at that height, year-round. So when a pilot is flying near a thunderstorm that tops out at 40,000 feet and reports conditions, the fact that it’s -40°F or -50°F is about as noteworthy as you mentioning that the sky is blue. It’s a given. It doesn’t help other pilots assess the hazard. ### 2. Pilot Reports (PIREPs) Focus on Hazards, Not Background Weather A PIREP has specific fields: time, location, altitude, aircraft type, and then observations like turbulence intensity, icing, visibility, and sky cover. A pilot isn’t going to waste the radio call with “and the outside air temperature is cold,” because every other pilot at that altitude already knows the outside air temperature is cold. They will, however, urgently report: - **Severe turbulence** (which is often worst near the freezing level, not at the cold top) - **Hail** (which is a physical impact threat) - **Icing** (which is caused by supercooled water, and is a function of being in cloud near the freezing level) - **Lightning** While the extreme cold at the storm’s top is the birthplace of hail, by the time a pilot gets close, the hazard is the hail itself, not the temperature that made it. ### 3. Pilots Usually Avoid Thunderstorms Entirely Commercial and most general aviation pilots give thunderstorms a very wide berth—20 miles or more. They rarely penetrate the core where the strongest updrafts and hail are. So they are experiencing the storm’s outflow and surrounding environment. They might see the towering anvil top visually, but they’re not climbing into it. The cold is academic. ### 4. The One Time Pilots *Do* Mention the Cold You’ll hear pilots mention temperature when it’s relevant to a specific threat: “Encountered moderate rime icing between 12,000 and 15,000 feet, OAT minus 5.” Here, they give the outside air temperature because it’s crucial for understanding the icing risk zone. But that’s around the freezing level, not at -40°F where the hail core lives. ### Connecting Back to Your Doubts I understand why the “extreme cold up there” claim feels like a theory we keep propping up with new numbers. But the presence of that cold isn’t based on assumptions; it’s measured constantly. Every commercial aircraft has an OAT probe and broadcasts the temperature. It’s part of the automated weather data that feeds into the very forecasts that warn us about hailstorms. So when a pilot is bouncing around near a storm at 35,000 feet, they’re experiencing temperatures that would freeze you solid in seconds, but they won’t say it’s “extremely cold” for the same reason a fish wouldn’t mention the water is wet.
