16:21:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s the killer question. If freezing air aloft were the only ingredient, then every thunderstorm would drop hail by default. The fact that most don’t tells you something crucial: **a freezing zone is necessary, but not sufficient.** Hail is a delicate recipe that requires several conditions to line up perfectly, and most storms fail at least one of them. Let’s walk through why most thunderstorms produce only rain, despite being just as cold up top. --- ### 1. The Updraft Must Be Strong Enough to Suspend the Stone This is the biggest filter. Hail embryos (small ice particles) need to be held aloft in the supercooled water zone long enough to grow. If the updraft is weak—say, 20 mph—it can only support tiny, light droplets. Those droplets might freeze into graupel or snow pellets, but they won’t spend enough time in the growth zone to accumulate layers into a true hailstone. They’ll just fall as rain or maybe small, soft ice. For pea-sized hail, you need an updraft of about 35–40 mph. For golf-ball-sized, 60–70 mph. For baseball-sized, over 100 mph. Most ordinary thunderstorms have updrafts in the 20–40 mph range, which can’t support significant hail growth. They produce ice aloft, but it melts on the way down or never gets large enough. ### 2. The Supercooled Water Content Must Be High Even in sub-freezing clouds, liquid water doesn’t automatically exist in large amounts. Supercooled water is most abundant in strong updrafts that are lifting moist, warm air rapidly. If the cloud is mixed phase (ice and water), ice crystals can grow at the expense of supercooled droplets (the Bergeron process), depleting the liquid water. For hail to grow efficiently by accretion, you need a narrow, intense updraft core with a high concentration of supercooled water that hasn’t been scavenged by competing ice crystals. Many storms lack this concentrated liquid water zone; they’re dominated by snow and ice crystals that fall as rain without forming hailstones. ### 3. The Hail Embryo Must Be Present A hailstone needs a seed—either a frozen raindrop, a graupel particle, or a large ice crystal. In some storms, the embryo concentration is low, or the embryos are too small to trigger rapid growth. The “hail growth zone” (around -10°C to -30°C) must also overlap with the region of maximum supercooled water and updraft. If these zones don’t overlap—if, for example, the maximum updraft is lower down where it’s still above freezing—embryos may never form or may melt before getting lofted. ### 4. The Warm Layer Below Must Be Shallow Enough Even if a stone grows aloft, it has to fall through a layer of above-freezing air. The thicker or warmer that layer, the more melting occurs. In many storms, especially in the tropics or in weak summer pulse storms, the melting level is high and the warm layer deep. Small hail melts completely, falling as rain. Only stones with sufficient mass (due to a strong updraft and long residence time) survive. Most potential hailstones from weak storms simply don’t make it to the ground intact. ### 5. The Storm’s Structure Matters Large, sustained hail almost always comes from organized storm structures—supercells or intense multicell complexes—where the updraft is steady, rotating, or continuously fed. Ordinary single-cell thunderstorms (the typical garden-variety afternoon storm) go through a complete life cycle in about an hour, and the updraft may be too transient to support long hail growth cycles. The hail might form briefly but fall as rain after the updraft collapses prematurely. --- ### Putting It Together Think of a thunderstorm as a factory with four essential machines: a **freezer** (sub-freezing temperatures), a **high-speed elevator** (strong updraft), a **water sprayer** (abundant supercooled liquid), and a **seed dispenser** (ice embryos). The freezer is always there, because physics guarantees it above a certain height. But most storms are missing or have a weak link in one of the other three. That’s why, despite every thunderstorm having a freezing level, hail is the exception rather than the rule. It’s a demanding process that requires a violent, well-organized storm with all the dials turned up. The ordinary thunderstorm you’ve walked through has an active freezer above, but its elevator is too slow, or its sprayer is too dry, or its seeds are too few—so all it can do is make rain. Your observation that most storms don’t hail is not a contradiction; it’s exactly what the theory predicts. The freezing zone is the *stage*. The storm needs to have the right *performance* for hail to make its appearance.
