t, 2016–2018), which carried new cloud particle imagers and OAT sensors into hail cores. - **In Europe, the SAFIRE ATR-42 and FAAM BAe-146** have conducted hailstorm penetrations with similar instrumentation, confirming the sub-freezing growth environment in Mediterranean storms. These missions all corroborate: at the altitude where hail is growing, it’s below freezing, and supercooled liquid water is present. #### 2. Dual-Polarization Radar (Widespread Since ~2011) This is revolutionary. The entire U.S. NEXRAD radar network was upgraded to dual-polarization. This allows forecasters to distinguish between rain, hail, and mixed precipitation in real time, *inside* the cloud. Specific signatures like **differential reflectivity (ZDR)** and **correlation coefficient (CC)** can pinpoint large hail and even estimate its size. Critically, radar can measure the height of the melting layer (the "bright band") and the top of the hail growth zone. Every time a giant hailstone is detected aloft, it’s above the melting layer in sub-freezing air. This is operational, every-day evidence—not a one-off research flight. Thousands of storms have been scanned, and the pattern holds universally: significant hail echoes only appear at altitudes where radiosonde data and numerical models say the temperature is below 0°C, with the largest stones forming in the -10°C to -30°C layer. #### 3. Dropsondes and Vertical Soundings (Daily and Ubiquitous) The freezing level is not inferred from a few balloons; it’s measured **every day, twice a day, at over 90 stations across the U.S.**, plus hundreds worldwide. These soundings form the backbone of every hail forecast. When a hail event occurs, the observed freezing level from the nearest morning sounding is used to verify the melting-layer depth. The system would have collapsed decades ago if the freezing level didn’t correlate with hail size—but it does, with remarkable reliability. #### 4. Wind Tunnel and Laboratory Experiments Hail growth is di